---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import '../styles/prose.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const startedHref = `${base}getting-started/`;
const blogHref = `${base}blog/`;
const architectureHref = `${base}architecture/`;

const description =
  'The vision behind Retro-Active: a community ecosystem for retro computing builders.';
---

<PageLayout title="About" description={description}>
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>The Vision</h1>

    <p>
      Retro-Active is for hobbyists who want to build a <em>new</em> computer
      in the spirit of the 8-, 16-, or even 32-bit machines that defined
      early personal computing. Maybe you&rsquo;re doing it to teach yourself
      how computers really work. Maybe you&rsquo;re doing it for the sheer
      joy of wiring up a CPU, some RAM, and a bus and watching it boot.
      Maybe you&rsquo;re finally building the machine you always wanted as
      a kid. All of those are the point.
    </p>

    <p>
      The catch: every builder ends up reinventing the same wheels &mdash; bus
      timing, memory decoders, interrupt glue, expansion options, driver
      stacks. Brilliant work gets done, then stays locked inside one project.
      The next builder starts from scratch. Retro-Active is a community
      ecosystem that tries to stop that. It gathers independent builders
      around a set of shared standards so the work each of us does can plug
      into the work everyone else does.
    </p>

    <h2>Why a Common Bus?</h2>

    <p>
      A bus is just an agreement: here are the pins, here is what the
      signals mean, here is the timing. When that agreement is shared, the
      community unlocks enormous leverage. One driver written for a
      keyboard card works across every machine that speaks the bus.
      One video card, designed once, can drop into a 6502 homebrew, a 68k
      board, or a soft-core system. Someone builds a better SD controller
      and suddenly every retro-active machine has better storage.
    </p>

    <p>
      The common bus isn&rsquo;t about locking anyone in. It&rsquo;s about
      giving independent builders a cheap way to share their
      work <em>without</em> coordinating every detail in advance.
    </p>

    <h2>One Bus, Many CPUs</h2>

    <p>
      You might be thinking: <em>how can a 6502 and a 68000 and a 386 all
      share the same bus? They have different pinouts, different word
      widths, different timing.</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      They can, because the Retro-Active bus isn&rsquo;t a projection of any
      one CPU. Early home-computer expansion slots often were &mdash; the
      Apple II slot, the original IBM PC ISA bus, and a lot of homebrew 68k
      buses are essentially the host CPU&rsquo;s pins brought out to a
      connector. That makes cards simple but locks the bus to one CPU
      forever. Our approach is closer in spirit to <strong>PCI</strong>: the
      bus is a defined protocol with its own signals, its own timing, and
      its own rules. Each host CPU connects through a small
      <em>bridge</em> &mdash; a handful of 74-series chips, a PAL/GAL, or a
      simple FPGA block &mdash; that translates the CPU&rsquo;s native cycles
      into bus transactions. The bridge is the only CPU-specific part.
      Everything past it &mdash; cards, drivers, software &mdash; stays the
      same.
    </p>

    <p>
      We&rsquo;re not building PCI. The complexity target matches the era:
      simpler timing, slower clocks, smaller address spaces, and bridges you
      can draw on a napkin. But the core idea &mdash; a CPU-independent
      protocol with a bridge per host &mdash; is what makes an 8-bit card
      work on a 32-bit machine, and what lets one driver compiled for many
      CPU families target them all.
    </p>

    <h2>Many Ways to Implement</h2>

    <p>
      How you build your cards is up to you. The bus is deliberately simple
      enough that you can implement it with plain 74-series logic, with
      PAL/GAL chips if you love that era, or with an FPGA if you want the
      flexibility to pack a lot of functionality onto one board. Some
      builders consider FPGAs cheating &mdash; that&rsquo;s a perfectly
      valid position, and our goal is to keep the bus and the software
      stack friendly to pure-logic designs. Others will reach for an
      ECP5 or ICE40 the moment they need something dense. Both camps
      ship cards that interoperate. That&rsquo;s the whole point.
    </p>

    <h2>Portable Software, Eternal Hardware</h2>

    <p>
      Hardware is only half the story. The other half is the software
      that runs on it. GCC&rsquo;s retro back-ends (68k, 6502, 8086, Z80,
      and the rest) let us write drivers and applications in C and C++
      and retarget them across families. FreeDOS, EmuTOS, and other free
      retro operating systems mean that &ldquo;boot to a real OS&rdquo; is not
      a preservation problem &mdash; it&rsquo;s a compile job.
    </p>

    <p>
      Together, those tools let a builder pick a CPU family, design a
      new system around it, and still pull in decades of existing retro
      software: recompile a vintage application for <em>your</em> system,
      port an old driver to a new card, and everything keeps working.
    </p>

    <h2>Many Paths, One Community</h2>

    <p>
      Retro-Active doesn&rsquo;t require a single philosophy. A
      74-series purist wire-wrapping a new 8-bit machine, a PAL/GAL
      enthusiast trimming their chip count, an FPGA hobbyist packing a
      whole system into one ECP5, and a hybrid builder mixing a real
      6502 with modern glue all belong here. Each of them benefits when
      someone in one camp publishes a bus decoder, a driver, or a boot
      ROM that the other camps can pick up and use.
    </p>

    <p>
      The goal is a rising tide &mdash; shared standards and shared code
      so that everyone&rsquo;s project gets richer, faster, and more
      reliable, without anyone having to change how they like to build.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to Start</h2>

    <p>
      If this sounds like your kind of project, head over to the
      <a href={startedHref}>Getting Started</a> guide for on-ramps tailored
      to discrete-logic builders, FPGA builders, and software developers.
      The <a href={blogHref}>blog</a> has deeper dives into specific
      decisions, and the <a href={architectureHref}>architecture</a> page
      has the current state of the bus standard.
    </p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
